{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795518596,"nonce":"6AQ3jfkzSKQLL6o1YhwdYn8xF4I=","message":"Where a fast swipe's CPU goes, at 6dc56707. cn_demo release-debug window, scripted swipe `BLINC_BENCH_WHEEL=.cn-calendar-months BLINC_BENCH_WHEEL_PERIOD=14` (a swipe every 0.23 s, so turns overlap as in continuous swiping). M1 Pro, 120 Hz display.\n\nProcess CPU over the swipe phase, from `ps` CPU time: period 36 (a swipe every 0.6 s) 11.7%, period 14 30.0% (31.0% with the profiler attached). The physical Magic Mouse sends more scroll events per swipe than the script, so a higher reading by hand is expected.\n\nTime Profiler, 15 s, all threads, 4366 samples:\n- Metal's own queue thread: 1896 (43%). Submitting command buffers (`_submitAvailableCommandBuffers` into IOGPU, about 556) and their completion handlers (about 383).\n- Main thread with wgpu on the stack: about 1150 (26%). Pass recording, `-[AGXG13XFamilyCommandQueue commandBuffer]`, render encoder creation, `get_current_texture`, submit, and the allocations and drops wgpu makes while recording.\n- Main thread, Blinc's own work: about 1270 (29%). Structural rebuild (`check_rebuild`) 363, full paint of structural frames (`render_tree_frame`) 369, hit testing on mouse moves about 200, the rest per-frame bookkeeping.\n\nSo about 70% of the process CPU is the cost of handing frames to the GPU, not of drawing on the CPU.\n\nMetal System Trace, 6 s: 784 frames (about 131 a second while the swipes run). 725 frames are slide frames with 12 encoders each; 56 are structural frames with 19 or 23. Each encoder sits in its own MTLCommandBuffer, 12.7 a frame, about 1,650 a second. GPU busy time is about 18% of the interval, so the GPU is not the limit.\n\nA slide frame's encoders: 3 Blinc Layer Primitives Pass, 2 Tight Blit Pass, 1 Blinc Overlay Render Pass, and about 6.4 Blit Command (the transit command buffers). The layer passes and tight blits are the two month grids drawn again into offscreen opacity layers every frame, because their keyframes fade them, although their content does not change during the slide.\n\nWhy the count is per pass: wgpu-core 26 records each render pass into its own hal command buffer and inserts a second one before it for the pass's transitions (`CommandEncoder::close_and_swap`, `open_pass`), and wgpu-hal's Metal `begin_encoding` makes an MTLCommandBuffer each time. Sharing one wgpu encoder per frame (uploads.rs) saves wgpu work but does not reduce Metal command buffers.\n\nLevers, in order of reach:\n1. Keep a fading group's layer texture while its content is unchanged and only composite it with the new opacity and translation. Removes the 2 layer passes and 2 tight blits per slide frame for the calendar, about 8 of the 12.7 command buffers.\n2. Fewer passes per frame in general, for example drawing the overlay content in the same pass as the frame's first draw.\n3. The structural frames and hit testing, the Blinc share, as 916db13 tracks.","files":null}]}